The name of the function may need to be changed though, to maybe something like `enable_focused_border_when_clicked`, so it's more clear.

Continuation of #1481. Relates to #1210. Replaces #1697 due to DCO causing trouble.

---

This PR contains a refactoring of the `MetricsUpdateTask` to improve its resource efficiency and accelerate its execution. The task is currently very resource intensive and runs for too long for large portfolios.

Calculating portfolio metrics in general is more I/O than CPU heavy and benefits greatly from parallelization (to a certain degree). For this to work out OK, the footprint of the work being parallelized should be as slim as possible in order to keep the impact on overall resource consumption manageable. Speed of metrics updates also mainly depends on how beefy the database server is.

I investigated on doing all this entirely in the database using views and stored procedures, but ultimately decided that it'd be too much of a hassle to maintain and test for all supported database technologies, which all have different syntax in certain areas. It would've been feasible if we only supported a single database, but that is not the reality today. 

The `PortfolioMetricsUpdateTask` now fetches a certain number (= number of CPU cores for now) of projects, and emits a `ProjectMetricsUpdateEvent` for each of them. It then waits for all events in the batch to be processed, and then repeats the process. This makes concurrent processing possible, leveraging the existing event system, *and* not overloading the event bus with too many events. 

---

## Benchmark

### Setup

#### Host system

* OS: macOS 12.5.1
* CPU: M1 Pro (10 Core)
* RAM: 8GB assigned to JVM
* JRE: Temurin 17.0.4+8 (aarch64)
* Database: PostgreSQL 14.5 (via [Postgres.app](https://postgresapp.com/))

In #1481 I used Docker Desktop to spin up a PostgreSQL database. I later found out that Docker Desktop has very poor Host -> Container network performance. Using a PostgreSQL running directly on the host system this time allows me to work with much larger datasets.

#### Test Data

* 1000 projects
* 146 components per project
* 146000 components in total

Dump of the test database: [nscur0_localhost-2022_08_27_20_24_37-dump.sql.gz](https://github.com/DependencyTrack/dependency-track/files/9448009/nscur0_localhost-2022_08_27_20_24_37-dump.sql.gz)

(Compressed with zstd, use `zstd -d nscur0_localhost-2022_08_27_20_24_37-dump.sql.gz -o dump.sql` to decompress).

### Execution

To isolate the functionality as much as possible, the following test class was profiled 3x per implementation, using IntelliJ's Async Profiler:

```java
public class MetricsUpdateTaskBenchmarkTest {

    @BeforeClass
    public static void setUpClass() {
        // (Not necessary for current master)
        EventService.getInstance().subscribe(ProjectMetricsUpdateEvent.class, ProjectMetricsUpdateTask.class);
        EventService.getInstance().subscribe(CallbackEvent.class, CallbackTask.class);
    }

    @AfterClass
    public static void tearDownClass() {
        // (Not necessary for current master)
        EventService.getInstance().unsubscribe(ProjectMetricsUpdateTask.class);
        EventService.getInstance().unsubscribe(CallbackTask.class);
    }

    @Test
    public void test() {
        final Properties jdoProps = JdoProperties.get();
        jdoProps.setProperty(PropertyNames.PROPERTY_CONNECTION_URL, "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/nscur0");
        jdoProps.setProperty(PropertyNames.PROPERTY_CONNECTION_DRIVER_NAME, org.postgresql.Driver.class.getName());
        jdoProps.setProperty(PropertyNames.PROPERTY_CONNECTION_USER_NAME, "nscur0");
        jdoProps.setProperty(PropertyNames.PROPERTY_CONNECTION_PASSWORD, "");

        final var pmf = (JDOPersistenceManagerFactory) JDOHelper.getPersistenceManagerFactory(jdoProps, "Alpine");
        PersistenceManagerFactory.setJdoPersistenceManagerFactory(pmf);

        new PortfolioMetricsUpdateTask().inform(new PortfolioMetricsUpdateEvent());
        // For current master:
        // new MetricsUpdateTask().inform(new MetricsUpdateEvent(MetricsUpdateEvent.Type.PORTFOLIO));
    }

}
```

### Results

For simplicity, these results contain *everything* captured by the profiler.  
This includes overhead introduced by JUnit and Alpine.

#### Current `master`

Total Duration | Total CPU Samples | Total Bytes Allocated
-- | -- | --
150s | 58'982 | 51'604'351'576
146s | 57'949 | 51'625'825'224
146s | 57'923 | 51'963'509'056

#### This PR

Total Duration | Total CPU Samples | Total Bytes Allocated
-- | -- | --
34,9s | 82'259 | 19'378'466'920
34,6s | 86'231 | 19'329'077'696
34,7s | 87'573 | 19'367'332'408

#### This PR with only 1 project processed concurrently ([`BATCH_SIZE`](https://github.com/DependencyTrack/dependency-track/pull/1704/files#diff-516633c841204e10369c28c86fb61b65dd21d8d9af0e8a82aba5a7f4c2194c0bR52) = 1)

I was curious what would happen if I reduced the number of projects being processed concurrently to 1, while keeping everything else the same. Didn't do much thorough testing with this, but it's still significantly faster and resource efficient than the current implementation:

Total Duration | Total CPU Samples | Total Bytes Allocated
-- | -- | --
82s | 29'481 | 19'360'286'792

---

### Other Changes

`MetricsUpdateEvent` and `MetricsUpdateTask` have been split up into four separate pairs:
* `ComponentMetricsUpdateEvent` + `ComponentMetricsUpdateTask`
* `ProjectMetricsUpdateEvent` + `ProjectMetricsUpdateTask`
* `PortfolioMetricsUpdateEvent` + `PortfolioMetricsUpdateTask`
* `VulnerabilityMetricsUpdateEvent` + `VulnerabilityMetricsUpdateTask`

Besides the code being a little more readable, this also contributes towards greater observability (#1796). It's now possible to see *what kind* of metrics events are being published, instead of just `MetricsUpdateEvent` for everything.

